+# arXiv:0906.1849 — coupled-XOR 3-SAT generator
+
+This is a locally verified, provisional Track B family. The generator and all exact
+gates pass, but the required four-vendor oracle run could not score an attempt because
+the supplied OpenRouter key returned HTTP 403 `Key limit exceeded`. The error-only
+transcripts are retained; this result is not release-ready until those runs complete.
+
+## Profile
+
+| field | value |
+|---|---|
+| Track | B — an efficient reference algorithm is disclosed |
+| Native domain | logic |
+| Object regime | finite discrete |
+| Computational core | CSP/SAT |
+| Certificate | integer tuple: one Boolean bit per variable |
+| Intended intuition | invariant: four clauses encode a parity row and the coupled operator is an involution |
+| Domain essentiality | native |
+| Reduction | none |
+
+## Problem and provenance
+
+The solver receives an ordinary 3-CNF formula and must return any satisfying Boolean
+assignment. A signed integer `j` denotes `xj`, and `-j` denotes `NOT xj`; checking a
+candidate is just exact Boolean evaluation of every clause. The source paper is
+[Ghosh and Misra, *A Randomized Algorithm for 3-SAT*](https://arxiv.org/abs/0906.1849).
+Section 1 fixes the CNF, literal, clause, assignment, and critical-clause definitions,
+and displays 3-variable XOR formulae as a tight example for PPZ. Theorem 3.1 analyzes
+that XOR example; Theorem 3.2 states the DEL–PPZ expected-time bound.
+
+The instance is built before it is solved. For `n=2m`, a random directed cycle `p`
+defines a binary matrix
+
+```text
+(Ax)_i     = x_i     XOR x_p(i) XOR x_(m+p(i))
+(Ax)_(m+i) = x_(m+i) XOR x_p(i) XOR x_(m+p(i)).
+```
+
+Writing `A=I+N` gives `N^2=0` over GF(2), hence `A^2=I`. The generator samples `x`,
+computes `r=Ax`, relabels the variables, and expands each parity row into four 3-CNF
+clauses. Balanced decoy pairs are satisfied by construction. Thus the core is
+invertible and has exactly one solution; `make_instance` never invokes a solver.
+
+## Why Track B
+
+This is not a Track A claim. Section 1 says 2-SAT is linear-time, so clauses of size at
+most two were avoided. The paper's disjoint XOR triples were also avoided because they
+separate into hand-sized components. For general 3-SAT, Algorithm 1 (PPZ) and Theorem
+3.2 give exponential randomized routes. For this generated distribution, however,
+grouping equal unsigned triples exposes XOR rows and exact GF(2) Gaussian elimination
+solves every instance in `O(M log M+n^3)` time.
+
+At the hard preset that reference algorithm solved 8/8 instances in 0.005557 seconds
+mean (0.012271 seconds maximum), using 26,145 counted scalar operations mean (27,419
+maximum). This is fast on a computer and far beyond unaided hand execution. Once the
+involution is recognized, the compact route is `x=Ar`, exactly 160 binary XORs for the
+80 output bits. That gap—not computational intractability—is the Track B hardness claim.
+
+## Worked demo (`seed=7`)
+
+This is the complete rendered demo instance; a person can solve it on paper by grouping
+the four clauses with each unsigned triple and doing six small parity equations.
+
+```text
+Find a satisfying assignment for the following 3-CNF formula.
+
+There are Boolean variables x1 through x6. A positive signed integer j
+means the literal xj; a negative signed integer -j means NOT xj.
+Each displayed line is one clause: the OR of its three literals. The
+whole formula is the AND of every clause. Variable indices are 1-based.
+Every answer entry must be exactly 0 (false) or 1 (true).
+
+n = 6; number of clauses = 24
+Clauses:
+C0001: -6 +3 -1
+C0002: -4 +1 +5
+C0003: +5 -3 +2
+C0004: +6 +2 -1
+C0005: -2 -3 -5
+C0006: -6 -1 -2
+C0007: -2 +3 -4
+C0008: -4 -5 +6
+C0009: +5 -2 +3
+C0010: +4 -5 -6
+C0011: +2 +3 +4
+C0012: +3 +6 +1
+C0013: -6 +1 +2
+C0014: -1 -5 -4
+C0015: +5 +4 +6
+C0016: +6 -1 -3
+C0017: -4 -3 +2
+C0018: +1 -3 -6
+C0019: +4 +1 -5
+C0020: -2 -3 +4
+C0021: +4 -1 +5
+C0022: -2 +1 +6
+C0023: -6 -4 +5
+C0024: -5 +3 +2
+
+Give your final answer inside <answer></answer> tags as one JSON list
+of exactly 6 bits in the order [x1,x2,...,x6].
+Example format: <answer>[0,1,0,1]</answer>
+The example only illustrates syntax; it does not have the required length.
+Output nothing else inside the tags.
+```
+
+The answer is `<answer>[1,1,0,0,1,0]</answer>`.
+`verify(inst, [1,1,0,0,1,0])` returns `(True, "ok")`. Flipping the first bit gives
+`[0,1,0,0,1,0]`, for which verification returns
+`(False, "clause 12 is unsatisfied")`.
+
+## Difficulty presets
+
+| preset | n | balanced decoy pairs | clauses | status |
+|---|---:|---:|---:|---|
+| demo | 6 | 0 | 24 | hand-solvable illustration |
+| easy | 24 | 24 | 144 | oracle run attempted; API failed before scoring |
+| medium | 48 | 96 | 384 | local gates pass; not reached by oracle |
+| hard | 80 | 240 | 800 | provisional shipping preset; all local gates pass |
+
+Difficulty first grows the hidden assignment space and then `escalate()` grows the
+balanced decoy haystack at fixed answer length. `SHIPPING_DIFFICULTY` is provisionally
+`hard`; it has not received the required harness verdict.
+
+## Gate results
+
+| gate | result |
+|---|---|
+| G1 | 12/12 planted witnesses verify |
+| G2 | 5/5 corruptions rejected with five distinct reasons |
+| G3 | tagged fenced JSON round-trips; answer is JSON-native |
+| G4 | 0/200,000 uniform admissible assignments valid; `2^80` language |
+| G5 | sampled shipping density 0/200,000; constructed exact count 1; reference 0.003056 s and 28,008 operations on the fixed measurement seed |
+| G6 | five attacks, each 0/8; reference GF(2) solver 8/8 |
+| G7 | doubled `n=160` instance builds and verifies; space grows from `2^80` to `2^160` |
+| G8 | 20/20 composed relabellings invariant, 20/20 carried witnesses valid, 20/20 unrelated keys distinct |
+| G9(c) | 161 characters, about 41 tokens, 80 atoms, 160 intended XORs — within every cap |
+
+The G6 failures are literal-majority outliers, public-order alternation, greedy clause
+repair for `2n` flips, 512 uniform restarts, and a plausible in-context ansatz that
+extracts parity rows but merely votes on their right-hand sides. Positive and negative
+literal counts tie exactly because both core and decoy blocks are sign-balanced.
+
+## Oracle loop
+
+No oracle response was scored. The bare harness wrote these records before refusing to
+make a hardness claim:
+
+| preset | seed | model | result |
+|---|---:|---|---|
+| easy | 842227164 | x-ai/grok-4.6 | HTTP 403 key limit |
+| easy | 1217618834 | google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview | HTTP 403 key limit |
+| easy | 1522107882 | google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview | HTTP 403 key limit |
+| easy | 635479632 | x-ai/grok-4.6 | HTTP 403 key limit |
+
+`llm_loop_transcript.jsonl` and `.meta.json` are the untouched harness outputs. There is
+no `hardened`, `too_easy`, or `cap_bound` verdict.
+
+## G9 arms
+
+| arm | scored solved/attempts | API error calls | conclusion |
+|---|---:|---:|---|
+| bare | 0/0 | 4 | unavailable |
+| structural hint | 0/0 | 4 | unavailable |
+| placebo hint | 0/0 | 4 | unavailable |
+
+The hinted-minus-placebo difference is undefined with zero scored attempts and is
+stored as `0.0` only as a neutral machine-readable placeholder. No conclusion about
+the claimed invariant can be drawn. The answer-size and 160-operation measurements
+remain valid independently of the unavailable diagnostic.
+
+## Use
+
+From the repository root:
+
+```python
+import importlib.util
+spec = importlib.util.spec_from_file_location("g", "results/0906.1849/gen_0906_1849.py")
+g = importlib.util.module_from_spec(spec)
+spec.loader.exec_module(g)
+inst = g.make_instance(seed=123, **g.DIFFICULTY["hard"])
+candidate = inst["answer"]
+assert g.verify(inst, candidate) == (True, "ok")
+```
+
+After a successful hardening rerun, emit with:
+
+```bash
+scripts/emit.sh 0906.1849 20 hard
+```
+
+## Caveats
+
+This family becomes easy immediately for a solver that programmatically groups clauses
+and performs GF(2) elimination; that is intentional and why the label is Track B.
+`P(guess)=0/200,000` estimates the uniform prior over all syntactically valid n-bit
+answers. It does not model a clause-learning solver, a parity-aware prior, or a solver
+that sees the involution.
+
+No industrial CDCL solver, PPSZ implementation, repeated Schöning walk, or repeated
+DEL–PPZ implementation was available in the dependency-free module. The successful
+parity/Gaussian algorithm is stronger on this distribution and is measured openly.
+The canonical key exactly normalizes variable-number permutations and input ordering,
+but treats literal polarity as part of the signed formula rather than normalizing
+independent variable complementation. Finally, the mandatory multi-vendor and G9
+diagnostics are missing for an external quota reason; no hardness conclusion should be
+drawn from their error-only transcripts.
