+The family becomes easy for code that groups the parity blocks and performs GF(2)
+elimination; that is intentional. `P(guess)=0/200,000` concerns the uniform prior over
+all syntactically valid 80-bit answers. It does not model parity-aware reasoning; the
+pair-aware restart panel gives a stricter practical check but still samples only 512
+of `2^40` orientations.
 
-No industrial CDCL solver, PPSZ implementation, repeated Schöning walk, or repeated
-DEL–PPZ implementation was available in the dependency-free module. The successful
-parity/Gaussian algorithm is stronger on this distribution and is measured openly.
-The canonical key exactly normalizes variable-number permutations and input ordering,
-but treats literal polarity as part of the signed formula rather than normalizing
-independent variable complementation. Finally, the mandatory multi-vendor and G9
-diagnostics are missing for an external quota reason; no hardness conclusion should be
-drawn from their error-only transcripts.
+The compact arithmetic count excludes the 3,840 literal inspections and associated
+grouping, so a no-tool failure may still reflect bookkeeping volume. No industrial
+CDCL/PPSZ implementation, Schöning walk, or repeated DEL–PPZ implementation was run;
+the exact parity/Gaussian algorithm is stronger on this distribution. The canonical
+key normalizes variable renumbering and input ordering but treats literal polarity as
+part of the signed formula; full signed-CNF isomorphism is not attempted. Most
+importantly, the required four-vendor and G9 diagnostics remain unavailable because of
+the external quota failure, so the local measurements do not establish the final
+hardness requirement.
